21. Custom Pipes – NestJS (Notes)

1. What is a Custom Pipe?
A custom pipe is a user-defined pipe
Used when built-in pipes are not enough
Helps with custom validation or transformation

2. Why use Custom Pipes?
Business-specific validation
Reusable logic
Cleaner controller code

3. Pipe Interface
A custom pipe must implement:
PipeTransform


4. Basic Structure of a Custom Pipe
import { PipeTransform, Injectable } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class CustomPipe implements PipeTransform {
  transform(value: any) {
    return value;
  }
}


5. Example 1: Validate String Length
import { PipeTransform, Injectable, BadRequestException } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class NameValidationPipe implements PipeTransform {
  transform(value: string) {
    if (value.length < 3) {
      throw new BadRequestException('Name is too short');
    }
    return value;
  }
}


6. Using Custom Pipe
Method Level
@Post()
createUser(@Body('name', NameValidationPipe) name: string) {}


Parameter Level
@Get(':id')
findOne(@Param('id', NameValidationPipe) id: string) {}


Controller Level
@UsePipes(NameValidationPipe)
@Controller('users')
export class UserController {}


Global Level
app.useGlobalPipes(new NameValidationPipe());


7. Example 2: Transform Input Data
@Injectable()
export class UpperCasePipe implements PipeTransform {
  transform(value: string) {
    return value.toUpperCase();
  }
}


8. Pipe Execution Flow
Request → Custom Pipe → Controller → Service


9. Key Points (Exam Ready)
Custom pipes implement PipeTransform
Can throw exceptions for validation
Can transform incoming data
Reusable and injectable

10. Difference: Built-in Pipe vs Custom Pipe
Built-in Pipe
Custom Pipe
Provided by NestJS
Created by developer
Common use cases
Business logic
Ready to use
Fully customizable


11. One-Line Definition
A custom pipe in NestJS is a user-defined class that implements PipeTransform to validate or transform request data.

